Round 20 (2026-02-27)

What landed
- Files changed:
  - `clang/aot/emit.c`
- Goal of this round: start eliminating expression-position APP churn by lowering strict-safe `APP* -> REF` spines to direct compiled calls.

Emitter/runtime behavior changes
1) Added strict-demand expression helper in emitter:
- New helpers:
  - `aot_emit_collect_ref_spine(...)`
  - `aot_emit_try_demanded_ref_call(...)`
  - `aot_emit_node_demanded(...)`
- New lowering rule:
  - In demanded expression contexts, if the static term is `APP(...APP(REF f, aN)..., a1)`, emit local direct call code:
    - Build local arg array (`call_args`) in APP-spine order expected by AOT ABI (outer->inner slots).
    - Evaluate args in original structural order (inner->outer), then store into fixed indexes.
    - Call `aot_call_ref(f, stack, s_pos, call_base=*s_pos, call_args, &call_pos)`.
    - Rebuild residual APPs from remaining args in reverse index order (`j = call_pos .. 1`) so term shape matches interpreter APP discipline exactly.
- Safety guard:
  - The fast path is emitted under `if (STEPS_ITRS_LIM == 0)`.
  - Else branch falls back to existing structural emission for identical step-by-step behavior.

2) Demand-context wiring (kept conservative):
- Head `OP2` strict operand emission now uses `aot_emit_node_demanded(...)` for lhs/rhs.
- `aot_emit_node_demanded(...)` also recurses through nested `OP2` terms, so strictness propagates through OP2 trees only.
- Lazy contexts (constructor fields, generic APP in non-demanded positions, etc.) keep old behavior.

Why this matters
- Previous telemetry showed compiled-mode heap dominated by size-2 allocations.
- This change removes APP materialization for a strict-safe subset (OP2-demanded `REF` app spines) while preserving laziness in non-demanded contexts.
- The call uses isolated base (`call_base=*s_pos`) and local args, preventing accidental consumption of caller-owned runtime APP frames.

